<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1963</h1><p class="ref-lede">1963 in world history: 45 events, 7 births, 17 deaths, 4 works, 3 states founded and 8 that came to an end. 318 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a><a href="/years/1960">1960</a><a href="/years/1961">1961</a><a href="/years/1962">1962</a><a href="/years/1963" aria-current="page">1963</a><a href="/years/1964">1964</a><a href="/years/1965">1965</a><a href="/years/1966">1966</a><a href="/years/1967">1967</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-2">2 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Viet Cong defeat a larger South Vietnamese force at Ap Bac</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-delta">Mekong Delta</a> beat off a South Vietnamese attack backed by American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopters</a> and advisers. The battle exposed weaknesses in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Saigon</a> army and US optimism about the war.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-14">14 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">De Gaulle vetoes Britain's entry into the Common Market</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">At a press conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a> rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s application to join the EEC. He vetoed a second application in 1967, and Britain joined only in 1973.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-de-gaulle">Charles de Gaulle</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-8">8 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Troops fire on Buddhist protesters in Huế</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">South Vietnamese forces fired on crowds protesting a ban on Buddhist flags, killing nine. The Buddhist crisis that followed undermined President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngo-dinh-diem">Ngo Dinh Diem</a>, who was overthrown in November.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-11">11 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Thích Quảng Đức burns himself to death in Saigon</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Buddhist monk set himself alight at a busy junction to protest the Diem government's treatment of Buddhists. Malcolm Browne's photograph shocked the world.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-11">11 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">George Wallace stands in the schoolhouse door</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a>'s governor blocked the entrance to the University of Alabama to stop two Black students enrolling, then stood aside when federalised Guard troops arrived. Kennedy addressed the nation on civil rights that night.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-16">16 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Valentina Tereshkova becomes the first woman in space</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Soviet cosmonaut Valentina Tereshkova launched aboard Vostok 6 and orbited Earth 48 times in nearly three days. No other woman flew in space for 19 years.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-20">20 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US and Soviet Union agree to set up a hotline</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a>, the agreement created a direct teletype link between Washington and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Popularly called the 'red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a>', it was never a telephone.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-26">26 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Kennedy tells West Berlin 'Ich bin ein Berliner'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Speaking at Rathaus Schöneberg to a crowd of about 450,000, President Kennedy declared solidarity with the people of the walled city two years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> went up.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">James Meredith graduates from the University of Mississippi</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">James Meredith became the first Black graduate of Ole Miss. His enrolment in 1962 had provoked a deadly riot and required thousands of federal troops to enforce.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-22">22 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Joseph Walker flies the X-15 to the edge of space</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">NASA pilot Joseph Walker took the X-15 rocket plane to 107.8 km, beyond the 100 km boundary of space. The record for a winged aircraft stood until SpaceShipOne in 2004.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Martin Luther King gives his 'I Have a Dream' speech</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Speaking to some 250,000 people at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a>, King described his dream of a nation free from racial injustice. The march helped build support for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/moscow" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-30">30 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Washington-Moscow hotline goes into service</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A direct teleprinter link between the White House and the Kremlin began operating. Set up after the Cuban missile crisis, it aimed to prevent war by misunderstanding; it was never a red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-2">2 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">CBS Evening News becomes America's first half-hour network newscast</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Walter Cronkite's evening news on CBS expanded from 15 to 30 minutes, opening with an interview with President Kennedy. NBC followed within a week, setting the format of US network news.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-22">22 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">President Kennedy is assassinated in Dallas</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> was shot dead while riding in an open motorcade through Dealey Plaza in Dallas. Vice-President Lyndon Johnson was sworn in aboard Air Force One the same afternoon.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Doctor Who is first broadcast</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The BBC broadcast the first episode of Doctor Who, about a time-travelling alien in a police box. It became the longest-running science fiction television series in the world.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Jack Ruby shoots Lee Harvey Oswald on live television</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Nightclub owner Jack Ruby shot Lee Harvey Oswald, accused of killing President Kennedy, in the basement of Dallas police headquarters. Millions saw the shooting live on television.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Johnson sets up the Warren Commission</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">President Lyndon Johnson appointed a commission under Chief Justice Earl Warren to investigate Kennedy's assassination. Its 1964 report concluded that Lee Harvey Oswald acted alone.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-12">12 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Kenya gains independence with Jomo Kenyatta as prime minister</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> became independent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jomo-kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a>, jailed by the colonial authorities during the Mau Mau emergency, became prime minister and later the country's first president.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/march-on-washington.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/march-on-washington">March on Washington</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, held on 28 August 1963, was one of the largest political demonstrations in American history: some 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial to demand civil and economic rights for African Americans.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/white-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/white-revolution">White Revolution</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The White Revolution was a programme of reforms launched by Mohammad Reza Shah Pahlavi of Iran and approved by referendum in January 1963.</p></div></li></ol></section>
